  "meta": {
    "title": "The Governed Step \u2014 The Whole Enforcement Story",
    "quality_profile": "showcase",
    "views": [
      {
        "id": "c1",
        "label": "1 \u00b7 Can it afford this?",
        "focus": [
          "lease",
          "exhausted"
        ],
        "note": "The lease is checked before anything else. Out of steps, time or money ends the run rather than starting work it cannot finish."
      },
      {
        "id": "c2",
        "label": "2 \u00b7 What is it, and with what?",
        "focus": [
          "resolve",
          "inputs",
          "unknown",
          "dangling"
        ],
        "note": "The registry is refreshed and the id resolved; bindings become inputs. Both failures here are data the agent can read."
      },
      {
        "id": "c3",
        "label": "3 \u00b7 May it?",
        "focus": [
          "judge",
          "ask",
          "refused"
        ],
        "note": "Governance sees the effect profile. Allow proceeds, Ask parks the run for a human, Refuse means it is never invoked."
      },
      {
        "id": "c4",
        "label": "4 \u00b7 Do it",
        "focus": [
          "invoke",
          "raised",
          "portfail"
        ],
        "note": "Now the component runs. If it raises, that is data. If a port raises, your host is broken and the run ends."
      },
      {
        "id": "c5",
        "label": "5 \u00b7 Record it",
        "focus": [
          "observe"
        ],
        "note": "The output is stored under the step's handle and the events go out. Only now is the step on the record."
      }
    ],
    "subtitle": "Seven moves in one function, three possible answers, and two kinds of failure that mean different things."
  },
